const passiveCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Passive before active before renewable', note: 'The triad in order: passive design (form and fabric do the work — free), efficient systems (small loads), then renewables (cover what remains). Inverting the order — solar panels on a leaky building — wastes money on the wrong end.' },
    { math: '2 · The Passivhaus standard, quantified', note: 'Super-insulation, airtightness (≤0.6 air changes/hr, blower-door tested), heat-recovery ventilation (≥75% efficient), high-performance glazing, no thermal bridges. Result: heating demand ≤15 kWh/m²/yr — ~90% below typical stock; 40,000+ certified buildings across climates (the method travels).' },
    { math: '3 · Embodied carbon joins the ledger', note: 'Operational (bills) + embodied (making the materials): concrete and steel carry heavy upfront carbon; mass timber can sequester it. Whole-life carbon assessment is becoming code (the LCA lesson, mandatory) — the design question shifted from "runs efficiently" to "was worth building".' },
    { math: '4 · Retrofit is the real frontier', note: '80% of 2050\'s buildings already exist — deep retrofit (fabric, systems, heat pumps) at scale is the hardest sustainability problem in the built environment; EnerPHit (the retrofit standard), district heating, and facade-overcladding are the current toolkit (the energy lesson\'s demand side, wholesale).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/expert/sustainable-and-passive-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Sustainable design is the environmental lesson\'s whole apparatus applied to buildings:
    <strong>passive first, efficient second, renewable last — and the carbon counted from
    manufacture to demolition</strong>. The Passivhaus physics makes it measurable; the retrofit
    problem makes it urgent.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the triad order, the standard, embodied carbon, the retrofit frontier" widget="equationStep" config={passiveCfg} />

  <h2>The expert\'s honest ledger</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Certifications and their gravity:</strong> LEED, BREEAM, Passivhaus — frameworks that standardised measurement and occasionally rewarded boxes checked; Passivhaus\'s physics-first, performance-tested approach made it the purist\'s benchmark (verified in use, not on paper).</li>
    <li><strong>Comfort vs energy is a false war:</strong> passive buildings are MORE comfortable (stable temperatures, no draughts, filtered air) — the environmental-design lesson\'s adaptive comfort, delivered by fabric.</li>
    <li><strong>Net-zero buildings exist and net-negative arrived:</strong> the Bullitt Center (Seattle: 575-year "living building" design, energy-positive in a cloudy climate) proved the extreme standard buildable; costs falling with each successor — the EV lesson\'s cost-curve logic, in buildings.</li>
    <li><strong>The design workflow changed:</strong> energy modelling from the first sketch (the environmental-design lesson\'s simulation, moved to concept stage), carbon budgets per element, and materials passports for future reuse — the circular-economy lesson, written into spec sections.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Passivhaus Institut; the Bullitt Center case; RIBA/LETI carbon guidance; the environmental-science ladder\'s LCA and mitigation lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
